==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|la|docēns}}, present participle of {{m|la|doceō||to teach}}. In the meaning of a university grade, as used in some Central European countries, it is clipped version of [[private docent#English|private docent]], {{m|en|privat-docent}}, from {{der|en|de|Privatdozent}}, from {{der|en|de|Dozent}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈdəʊ.sənt/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-docent.wav|Audio (Berkshire)}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/ˈdoʊ.sənt/}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# [[instructive|Instructive]]; that teaches.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[teacher]] or [[lecturer]] at some [[universities]] (in central Europe, etc.)
#* '''2006''', Thomas Pynchon, ''Against the Day'', Vintage 2007, p. 1212:
#*: Zermelo had been a '''docent''' at Göttingen when Kit was there and, like Russell, had been preoccupied with the set of all sets that are not members of themselves.
# {{lb|en|chiefly|US}} A [[tour]] [[guide]] at a [[museum]], [[art gallery]], historical site, etc.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2020|author=[[w:Brit Bennett|Brit Bennett]]|title=[[w:The Vanishing Half|The Vanishing Half]]|publisher=Dialogue Books|page=149|passage=She was listening distractedly as an elderly '''docent''' intoned to a circle of listless children.}}
#: {{ux|en|The '''docent''' greeted the visitors and welcomed them to the Smithsonian.}}

